get an update now from Thailand what  could be the final rescue operation has  resumed with four boys and their  football coach remaining trapped  underground in the caves the authorities  say they are aiming to bring them all  out to safety today and we can speak to  our correspondent Sally Lockwood who's  in chiang rai and by definition sally if  you're trying to get five people out  rather than four in the two previous  successful rescue attempts this is going  to be even more nerve-racking  nerve-racking yes and one would think  more lengthy however rescue teams have  done very well in the last two days as  we've seen yesterday they improved their  time their rescue time by two hours it  took them nine hours to rescue the four  boys yesterday instead of the eleven  hours the day before so they are  obviously becoming more familiar with  what's involved and more confident  because they've been so successful so  far which is obviously buoyed everyone  here everyone who's been waiting for  this news anxiously not least the  families but the world is watching and  willing this to be a happy ending and so  far it's looking really good and we've  been getting reports in the last few  moments that the ninth boy has been  rescued we are awaiting confirmation on  this but we've been told by people here  on the ground we've been heading up to  the cave entrance that the night boy has  been rescued and we are hoping to see an  ambulance in the next 45 minutes around  five o'clock local time  it's obviously we have to be very  careful not to celebrate prematurely  here because the boys are going through  such an extraordinary ordeal in this  evacuation it's a full kilometer  evacuation from the shelter where  they've been from the ledge where  they've been sheltering for the last 17  nights now and it involves them having  to dive for parts of this escape on  their own and of course as we know these  boys had never dived before most of them  didn't swim and the expert diver